    "done_when": "Context: followup f-ctta-sweep-device-placement. The near-linear contour-tree sweep (nova/equilibrium/contour_tree.py, merged 4db6fb357) is launch-latency bound on the H200: a fori_loop of vertex visits, each a data-dependent while_loop, takes 2.772 s at 1089 vertices against 0.490 s on CPU. Deliverable: benchmarks/contour_tree_sweep_placement.py, which measures three placements on the twelve certificate rungs and the two MAST carriers that tests/test_contour_tree_brute_force.py builds, at batch sizes 1, 8 and 64. The placements are (a) one field host-side on CPU, (b) batched fields on the H200 with vmap over the existing sweep, and (c) a parallel union-find formulation on the device, written in the benchmark as a prototype and not in nova/. It reports compile and execute walls per placement and batch, and checks with benchmarks/contour_tree_brute_force.py --compare that every placement's receipt fields are bit-identical to the CPU single-field receipt. Evidence: the fragment at docs/evidence/fragments/contour-tree-topology-authority/ctta-sweep-device-placement.html, with one figure of execute wall against vertex count per placement (data-ink style, direct labels) and a recommended placement for each of three uses: single solve, labeller batch and census. Read a titan or H200 number as that device's number. Run on the H200 through RECKON_ALLOW_NESTED_LAUNCH=1 scripts/nova_lane/run.sh --rungs h200 --force-submit; the coordinator authorises this launch path, and the general fence does not forbid it. The CPU arm runs in place on this fleet allocation. No nova/ source change in this node. Every uv command carries --no-sync and nothing runs uv sync. Write the manifest before any long step, with empty wait fields unless a wait_probe names a job id and wait_terminal names a state the probe prints; its status is one of complete, blocked or failed.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Measure where the contour-tree sweep should run for single, batched and census use",
